//go:build go1.18

// mayhem harness — DELTA_BYTE_ARRAY column-encoding decoder.
//
// Fuzzes delta.ByteArrayEncoding.DecodeByteArray directly on arbitrary bytes,
// i.e. bytes an attacker fully controls as already-"encoded" column data
// inside a page (the whole-file target above gets here too, but only for
// pages that survive the footer/thrift/page-header gauntlet first — this
// target reaches the decoder directly so the fuzzer doesn't have to also
// construct a valid file around it).
//
// This is a NEW harness rather than a build of upstream's native
// FuzzDeltaByteArray (encoding/delta/delta_test.go): that native fuzz
// function has a two-argument signature (`func(t *testing.T, input []byte,
// seed int64)`) whose second argument only makes sense together with
// go-fuzz-headers' own byte-consumption format under go-118-fuzz-build, so a
// go-wrapper corpus file decoded to raw bytes wouldn't reproducibly seed both
// arguments. It also round-trips encode(generate(fuzz bytes)) then decode,
// which tests encoder/decoder agreement rather than decoder robustness
// against arbitrary (i.e. attacker-supplied) already-encoded bytes. Calling
// DecodeByteArray directly on the fuzz bytes is simpler, needs only a single
// []byte seed, and targets the actual security-relevant surface: decoding
// untrusted column data.
//
// The encoding itself already guards the classic "declared count says
// millions of values" resource-exhaustion trap (see
// encoding/delta/binary_packed.go's maxSupportedBlockSize), so no extra
// bounding is added here.
//
// No file I/O: bytes come only from the fuzzer; starter seeds are wired via
// the Mayhemfile `testsuite:` directive.
//
// Committed as `.go.src` so it is never picked up as an ordinary package file
// by `go build`/`go test`. mayhem/build.sh copies it into a FRESH,
// single-file package under `_mayhem_harness/` (the leading underscore makes
// every Go tool — build, vet, `go test ./...` — ignore it automatically for
// wildcard patterns) for the go-118-fuzz-build step, which is given the
// directory explicitly and so still finds it. It is NOT copied into
// encoding/delta/: that package directory already mixes `package delta` and
// `package delta_test` files (upstream ships both), and go-118-fuzz-build's
// package loader rejects a directory containing two different package names.
// A fresh single-package directory sidesteps that entirely.

package deltabytearray

import (
	"testing"

	"github.com/parquet-go/parquet-go/encoding/delta"
)

func FuzzMayhemDeltaByteArrayDecode(f *testing.F) {
	f.Fuzz(func(t *testing.T, data []byte) {
		enc := new(delta.ByteArrayEncoding)
		// Malformed/truncated encoded data is an expected outcome (returned
		// error), not a finding.
		_, _, _ = enc.DecodeByteArray(nil, data, nil)
	})
}
